# Template for .env, which is gitignored. Copy it before editing:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `docker compose up` needs no .env at all — every variable below has a
# working local default baked into docker-compose.yml. Set these when running
# outside compose (`bun run dev` against your own Postgres), or to override
# a default.
#
# Client-exposed variables must be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_, which inlines them
# into the JavaScript bundle. Anything secret must not carry that prefix.
# This file is committed and the repository is world-readable — placeholder
# values only, never real ones.

# Postgres. The compose stack creates this exact database; point elsewhere if
# you run your own. pgvector must be available as an extension.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://growthmind:growthmind@localhost:5432/growthmind

# Better Auth. The dev fallback is fine locally; generate a real secret for
# anything reachable from the internet:  openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=dev-only-secret-change-me-32-chars!
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Encryption at rest for the analytics credentials customers attach to a
# project. Base64 of 32 random bytes:  openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Outside production this has a working dev default, so a fresh clone and
# `docker compose up` need nothing here. In production it is required, and a
# second check refuses the published dev value when a customer credential is
# about to be stored — that check cannot be waived by
# GROWTHMIND_ALLOW_INSECURE_DEFAULTS, because this key protects someone
# else's account rather than this deployment's own sessions. Boot still
# succeeds either way; attaching an analytics account is what stops.
# GROWTHMIND_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Model keys — optional, and always yours (product decisions §12: token spend
# is visible and on your budget). Nothing in the scaffold requires this yet;
# the analysis pipeline will degrade gracefully without it.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Which model the cold-start analysis lane asks for a written explanation.
# Optional, validated only if you set it, and NOTHING READS IT YET — the lane
# that will branch on it is not built. It is documented here because every
# variable the app validates belongs in this file, not because setting it does
# anything today. Leave it unset and the lane will use its own default when it
# lands; the findings themselves never depend on it, only the plain-English
# explanation attached to them.
# GROWTHMIND_COLDSTART_MODEL=

# PostHog, for local tooling only. These four are now validated if you set
# them (so a typo fails at startup rather than halfway through a script), but
# THE APP STILL NEVER READS THEM: a customer's PostHog credentials are stored
# per project, encrypted, and read from the database. A global key here would
# be a single-tenant design in a multi-tenant product, so it does not exist.
#
# What they are actually for: `bun scripts/spikes/*`. Leaving all four unset
# is fully supported everywhere, including production — a self-hosted install
# with no PostHog boots normally and says plainly what is unavailable.
#
# WARNING: point them at a throwaway TEST PostHog project — the spike
# harnesses write synthetic events into it.
#
# POSTHOG_HOST — your PostHog region URL, e.g. https://us.posthog.com
# POSTHOG_PROJECT_API_KEY — project API key (phc_...) from project settings;
#   used to capture (write) events. Not a secret, but use the test project's.
# POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY — personal API key (phx_...); a real secret. Used
#   to READ events/recordings; create it on PostHog's personal API keys page
#   with read scope for the test project.
# POSTHOG_PROJECT_ID — numeric project id from project settings.
# CHROME_PATH — optional; absolute path to a Chrome/Edge/Chromium binary for
#   the session-recording leg's headless browser. Auto-detected if unset.
# POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.posthog.com
# POSTHOG_PROJECT_API_KEY=phc_your_project_api_key
# POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY=phx_your_personal_api_key
# POSTHOG_PROJECT_ID=12345
# CHROME_PATH=
